WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:07.449
NARRATOR: Begin by framing Lesson Title:
Foundations of Cryptographic Systems as a

2
00:00:07.449 --> 00:00:11.765
question about inputs, decisions, and
observable results.

3
00:00:11.765 --> 00:00:17.466
NARRATOR: The course also provides an
introduction to data integrity and

4
00:00:17.466 --> 00:00:17.984
authentication.

5
00:00:17.984 --> 00:00:25.508
NARRATOR: Consider this worked case: AES
encryption uses a symmetric key to transform

6
00:00:25.508 --> 00:00:31.874
plaintext into ciphertext, ensuring only
authorized parties can read the message.

7
00:00:31.875 --> 00:00:39.622
NARRATOR: On screen, display a side-by-side
comparison of plaintext and ciphertext with a

8
00:00:39.622 --> 00:00:44.390
symmetric key lock icon, labeled
'Confidentiality via AES'.

9
00:00:44.390 --> 00:00:50.560
NARRATOR: List the givens before choosing a
method, because an unstated assumption can

10
00:00:50.560 --> 00:00:51.984
change the conclusion.

11
00:00:51.984 --> 00:00:57.656
NARRATOR: This establishes the vocabulary and
scope needed for the remaining sections.

12
00:00:57.770 --> 00:01:04.420
NARRATOR: Now define Lesson Title: Classical
Cryptosystems Overview precisely enough that

13
00:01:04.420 --> 00:01:08.048
another learner could test the definition.

14
00:01:08.048 --> 00:01:15.732
NARRATOR: It covers classical cryptosystems,
private-key cryptosystems (including DES and

15
00:01:15.732 --> 00:01:21.110
AES), hashing and public-key cryptosystems
(including RSA).

16
00:01:21.110 --> 00:01:28.241
NARRATOR: Consider this worked case: The
Caesar cipher shifts each letter by a fixed

17
00:01:28.241 --> 00:01:33.844
number, demonstrating how simple substitution
can be broken with frequency analysis.

18
00:01:33.844 --> 00:01:41.392
NARRATOR: On screen, show a Caesar cipher
alphabet wheel with 'A' shifting to 'D',

19
00:01:41.392 --> 00:01:43.548
annotated with 'Substitution Vulnerability'.

20
00:01:43.548 --> 00:01:50.380
NARRATOR: Separate the formal definition from
an implementation detail, then identify what

21
00:01:50.380 --> 00:01:52.657
evidence would distinguish them.

22
00:01:52.657 --> 00:01:59.344
NARRATOR: A precise definition makes later
examples easier to evaluate and revise.

23
00:01:59.450 --> 00:02:05.948
NARRATOR: With the foundation in place,
examine how Lesson Title: Private-Key

24
00:02:05.948 --> 00:02:09.493
Cryptosystems operates in a concrete sequence.

25
00:02:09.493 --> 00:02:15.806
NARRATOR: Encryption supports confidentiality
when keys and modes are used correctly.

26
00:02:15.806 --> 00:02:23.459
NARRATOR: Consider this worked case: DES and
AES require the same key for encrypting and

27
00:02:23.459 --> 00:02:29.071
decrypting data, making them fast but
dependent on secure key distribution.

28
00:02:29.071 --> 00:02:36.252
NARRATOR: On screen, illustrate a two-way
arrow between 'Sender' and 'Receiver' with a

29
00:02:36.252 --> 00:02:40.118
single key symbol, labeled 'Symmetric Key
Exchange'.

30
00:02:40.118 --> 00:02:47.290
NARRATOR: Follow every arrow in order and name
the state before and after each transition.

31
00:02:47.290 --> 00:02:52.899
NARRATOR: The trace converts an abstract
statement into an inspectable sequence.

32
00:02:52.899 --> 00:02:59.738
NARRATOR: Name each dependency explicitly, and
keep labels and representations consistent

33
00:02:59.738 --> 00:03:01.603
throughout the analysis.

34
00:03:01.706 --> 00:03:08.795
NARRATOR: Next, compare the choices involved
in Lesson Title: Public-Key Cryptosystems and

35
00:03:08.795 --> 00:03:11.749
make the decision criteria explicit.

36
00:03:11.749 --> 00:03:18.008
NARRATOR: The Secure Software Development
Framework groups practices into four practice

37
00:03:18.008 --> 00:03:18.577
groups.

38
00:03:18.577 --> 00:03:26.366
NARRATOR: Consider this worked case: RSA uses
a public key for encryption and a private key

39
00:03:26.366 --> 00:03:31.234
for decryption, solving the key distribution
problem in symmetric systems.

40
00:03:31.234 --> 00:03:39.292
NARRATOR: On screen, draw a split key icon:
'Public Key' (open hand) and 'Private Key'

41
00:03:39.292 --> 00:03:42.515
(closed fist), labeled 'Asymmetric Key Pair'.

42
00:03:42.515 --> 00:03:49.215
NARRATOR: Compare alternatives against the
same constraints so the evaluation does not

43
00:03:49.215 --> 00:03:50.890
move the goalposts.

44
00:03:50.890 --> 00:03:57.437
NARRATOR: Using shared criteria turns a
preference into a defensible technical choice.

45
00:03:57.554 --> 00:04:03.499
NARRATOR: Apply Lesson Title: Hashing
Functions to a worked situation instead of

46
00:04:03.499 --> 00:04:06.472
treating it as an isolated definition.

47
00:04:06.472 --> 00:04:12.484
NARRATOR: Produce Well-Secured Software
addresses security requirements, design,

48
00:04:12.484 --> 00:04:14.738
verification, and release.

49
00:04:14.738 --> 00:04:22.715
NARRATOR: Consider this worked case: SHA-256
generates a unique 256-bit hash for a

50
00:04:22.715 --> 00:04:28.238
document, where altering any bit changes the
entire output.

51
00:04:28.238 --> 00:04:37.847
NARRATOR: On screen, show a document entering
a hash function box, outputting a long hex

52
00:04:37.847 --> 00:04:41.691
string, labeled 'Integrity Check via SHA-256'.

53
00:04:41.691 --> 00:04:48.215
NARRATOR: Work from the concrete inputs to the
result, checking units, types, or invariants

54
00:04:48.215 --> 00:04:49.613
at each step.

55
00:04:49.613 --> 00:04:54.660
NARRATOR: The worked case shows both how to
apply the method and how to check it.

56
00:04:54.770 --> 00:05:01.263
NARRATOR: Examine the tradeoffs in Lesson
Title: Data Integrity and Authentication,

57
00:05:01.263 --> 00:05:05.985
including what improves and what becomes more
difficult.

58
00:05:05.985 --> 00:05:12.643
NARRATOR: Message authentication and digital
signatures address integrity and authenticity

59
00:05:12.643 --> 00:05:14.641
in different ways.

60
00:05:14.641 --> 00:05:21.020
NARRATOR: Consider this worked case: A digital
signature uses the sender's private key to

61
00:05:21.020 --> 00:05:26.032
sign a message, which the receiver verifies
with the public key.

62
00:05:26.032 --> 00:05:34.540
NARRATOR: On screen, display a document with a
signature stamp and a verification checkmark,

63
00:05:34.540 --> 00:05:37.579
labeled 'Authenticity via Digital Signatures'.

64
00:05:37.579 --> 00:05:43.864
NARRATOR: State the benefit, the cost, and the
condition under which the tradeoff is

65
00:05:43.864 --> 00:05:44.313
acceptable.

66
00:05:44.313 --> 00:05:51.501
NARRATOR: Making the tradeoff visible prevents
a local improvement from hiding a larger cost.

67
00:05:51.602 --> 00:05:58.243
NARRATOR: Stress-test the reasoning around
Lesson Title: Key Management Practices with an

68
00:05:58.243 --> 00:06:03.223
edge case and identify which assumption
controls the outcome.

69
00:06:03.223 --> 00:06:10.794
NARRATOR: Key management covers generation,
distribution, storage, use, rotation,

70
00:06:10.794 --> 00:06:13.317
revocation, and destruction.

71
00:06:13.317 --> 00:06:20.713
NARRATOR: Consider this worked case: Keys must
be stored in hardware security modules and

72
00:06:20.713 --> 00:06:24.411
rotated regularly to limit exposure if
compromised.

73
00:06:24.411 --> 00:06:31.420
NARRATOR: On screen, show a key lifecycle
diagram: 'Generate' -> 'Store' -> 'Use' ->

74
00:06:31.420 --> 00:06:35.926
'Rotate' -> 'Destroy', with lock icons at each
stage.

75
00:06:35.926 --> 00:06:41.912
NARRATOR: Change one assumption at a time and
observe which part of the reasoning no longer

76
00:06:41.912 --> 00:06:42.286
holds.

77
00:06:42.286 --> 00:06:48.671
NARRATOR: The edge case reveals the boundary
of the claim without discarding the useful

78
00:06:48.671 --> 00:06:49.583
core idea.

79
00:06:49.706 --> 00:06:56.995
NARRATOR: Use Lesson Title: Modes of Operation
to diagnose a failure by separating symptoms,

80
00:06:56.995 --> 00:06:59.077
causes, and corrective action.

81
00:06:59.077 --> 00:07:05.200
NARRATOR: Modes of operation define how a
primitive processes data larger than one

82
00:07:05.200 --> 00:07:05.671
block.

83
00:07:05.671 --> 00:07:12.803
NARRATOR: Consider this worked case: CBC mode
uses a chaining value to link blocks,

84
00:07:12.803 --> 00:07:15.859
preventing pattern repetition in encrypted
output.

85
00:07:15.859 --> 00:07:23.612
NARRATOR: On screen, illustrate a block cipher
processing two blocks with a feedback loop,

86
00:07:23.612 --> 00:07:25.827
labeled 'CBC Mode Chaining'.

87
00:07:25.827 --> 00:07:31.509
NARRATOR: Locate the first point where
observed behavior diverges from expected

88
00:07:31.509 --> 00:07:35.124
behavior, then test the smallest plausible
cause.

89
00:07:35.124 --> 00:07:41.062
NARRATOR: The diagnosis is strongest when
another person can reproduce the observation

90
00:07:41.062 --> 00:07:42.546
and the test.

91
00:07:42.650 --> 00:07:48.042
NARRATOR: Connect Lesson Title: Security
Implementation & Validation to the earlier

92
00:07:48.042 --> 00:07:52.943
ideas and explain how the pieces work as one
system.

93
00:07:52.943 --> 00:07:58.836
NARRATOR: Security depends on the complete
protocol and implementation, not only an

94
00:07:58.836 --> 00:07:59.818
algorithm name.

95
00:07:59.818 --> 00:08:07.571
NARRATOR: Consider this worked case: Timing
attacks can exploit variable execution times

96
00:08:07.571 --> 00:08:12.740
in cryptographic libraries, requiring
constant-time implementations for validation.

97
00:08:12.740 --> 00:08:20.990
NARRATOR: On screen, show a security checklist
with items: 'Algorithm Validated',

98
00:08:20.990 --> 00:08:26.240
'Implementation Tested', 'Side-Channel
Mitigated', labeled 'Validation Checklist'.

99
00:08:26.240 --> 00:08:35.165
NARRATOR: Explain each connection explicitly
and verify that information retains the same

100
00:08:35.165 --> 00:08:37.396
meaning across representations.

101
00:08:37.396 --> 00:08:44.564
NARRATOR: The synthesis matters only if every
connection remains supported by the original

102
00:08:44.564 --> 00:08:45.115
evidence.

103
00:08:45.218 --> 00:08:51.237
NARRATOR: Finish with Lesson Title: Practical
Analysis Checklist as a practical checklist

104
00:08:51.237 --> 00:08:54.246
that can guide a new analysis.

105
00:08:54.246 --> 00:09:00.386
NARRATOR: Cryptographic standards define
approved algorithms and parameters.

106
00:09:00.386 --> 00:09:09.256
NARRATOR: Consider this worked case: A secure
system uses AES for confidentiality, SHA-256

107
00:09:09.256 --> 00:09:16.761
for integrity, RSA for authentication, and
follows NIST key management standards.

108
00:09:16.761 --> 00:09:24.402
NARRATOR: On screen, display a practical
analysis checklist with checkboxes:

109
00:09:24.402 --> 00:09:29.750
'Confidentiality Mechanism', 'Integrity
Check', 'Authentication Method', 'Key

110
00:09:29.750 --> 00:09:32.042
Management', 'Implementation Validated'.

111
00:09:32.042 --> 00:09:39.685
NARRATOR: For each checklist item, record the
evidence, the remaining uncertainty, and the

112
00:09:39.685 --> 00:09:41.449
next verification step.

113
00:09:41.449 --> 00:09:47.390
NARRATOR: The result is a repeatable
procedure, not a conclusion that depends on

114
00:09:47.390 --> 00:09:48.761
memory or intuition.
